#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The speed script of S5 (rule parity: sqll passes 100% of the rule
fixtures, lint and fix; cases that need the Jinja templater move to the Jinja
templater stone).

Design record: docs/decisions/s5-rules.md, section 5 ("The speed rule for
each pull request"). Task T3 (goal of the task: each pull request of this
track that changes the lint and fix path gives the HEAD / BASE, HEAD /
sqlfluff, and HEAD / sqruff ratios of the lint and fix benchmarks for the
rules that sqll has) of S5 made this script.

  uv run --frozen --project harness python scripts/s5/speed run --label LABEL \\
      --scratch DIR [--base REF] [--head REF] [--image IMAGE]
  uv run --frozen --project harness python scripts/s5/speed report \\
      --label LABEL [--check]

The S2 benchmarks select all rules ("rules = all"), and sqll fails closed on
a rule that it does not have. Thus their lint and fix benchmarks of sqll are
"unsupported" until sqll has the 79 rules (harness/bench.md, "Add a tool").
This script runs the same benchmark inputs and settings with only the rules
that sqll has at BASE:

1. run takes the machine lock (/tmp/sqll-machine.lock; PROCESS.md,
   "Tracks", rule 7). It also accepts the lock from a parent "flock"
   command. If another process holds the lock, it stops (exit code 2): it
   does not wait. Use "flock /tmp/sqll-machine.lock scripts/s5/speed ..."
   to wait for the lock.
2. HEAD is the commit of --head (default: HEAD). BASE is the commit of
   --base (default: the merge base of HEAD and origin/main). For BASE, then
   HEAD: a git worktree, its submodule, and its own scripts/build-sqll, as
   scripts/speed-check does (its functions build_role and resolve).
3. One container of the CI image (no network, serial; the S2 method, as
   scripts/speed-check): the subcommand run-inside. It stops (exit code 2)
   if the machine lock of the host (mounted read-only) is free. Then:
   a. The tool check of each tool ("sqll-harness tools").
   b. The rules: the rules that sqll has at BASE ("sqll rules" of the BASE
      binary). Each tool must have each of them: sqll at HEAD ("sqll
      rules"), sqlfluff ("sqlfluff rules"), and sqruff ("sqruff rules").
      Else it stops: the tools cannot run the same rules.
   c. The settings of each tool: the S2 settings (harness/bench.md,
      "Settings") with core.rules = the rules, separated by ",".
   d. The inputs: the build of the S2 benchmark inputs must be that of the
      definitions (harness/sqll_harness/bench/definitions.json). Else it
      stops.
   e. The reference: the S2 sqlfluff reference (the rule codes of lint, and
      if fix changes the file) has all rules, so it is made again with the
      rules: sqlfluff lints and fixes each input once, with the checks of
      harness/bench.md, "Validity". A run that is not valid, or a rule code
      that the rules do not have, stops it.
   f. The lint and fix benchmarks of the 28 dialects and 3 sizes (168
      benchmarks), with the full profile, for sqll (HEAD), sqll-base
      (BASE), sqlfluff, and sqruff. The tools run in turn for each repeat.
      The validity checks compare each tool with the reference of step e.
4. The record goes to results/S5/speed/LABEL.json: the commits, the builds,
   the rules, the rules of each tool, the reference, the report, the steps,
   and the summary.

report reads only the committed record and makes the summary again. With
--check, it writes nothing and fails if the file is not the new text.

The summary. For each benchmark: the median wall time of each tool, and 3
ratios (ratio = the median wall time of HEAD / that of the other tool, 4
digits; null if a result is not valid): HEAD / BASE, HEAD / sqlfluff, and
HEAD / sqruff. HEAD / BASE also has the compare entry of "sqll-harness bench
compare" (the noise limit of harness/bench.md, "Slowdowns"). The groups (by
command and size, and the total) give the ratio of the sums of each pair.
The slower benchmarks:

  head_base       HEAD is slower than BASE (above the noise limit).
  failure         BASE is valid and HEAD is not.
  head_not_valid  HEAD is not valid.
  sqlfluff        HEAD / sqlfluff is 1 or more (G3: faster than sqlfluff on
                  each benchmark).
  sqruff          HEAD / sqruff is more than 1 (G3: not slower than sqruff
                  on any benchmark).
  sqruff_total    HEAD / sqruff of the total is 1 or more, or there is no
                  pair (G3: faster than sqruff on the total).

A benchmark where BASE, sqlfluff, or sqruff is not valid has no ratio for
that tool. It is in "no_pair" with the status and the reason.

Exit codes: 0 if there is no slower benchmark. 1 if there is one (the pull
request gives the reason; PROCESS.md, rule 8), or if report --check finds a
difference. 2 if there is no result: the machine lock is not taken, a tool
cannot run the same rules, an input is missing or is not that of the
definitions, a step failed, or the record does not agree with itself, the
definitions, or the S2 baseline (results/s2/baseline.json).
"""

